.TH F_CREATED_QUERY 2const 2026-10-11 "Linux man-pages (unreleased)"
.SH NAME
F_CREATED_QUERY
\-
determine whether a file was created
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library
.RI ( libc ,\~ \-lc )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #define _GNU_SOURCE
.B #include <fcntl.h>
.P
.BI int\~fcntl(int\~ fd ", F_CREATED_QUERY);"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A call to
.BR open (2)
with
.B O_CREAT
may optionally create a file,
if the specified file does not already exist.
If the file was created, the
.B F_CREATED_QUERY
operation on the resulting
.I fd
returns 1.
Note that this is a property of the
.IR "open file description" ,
rather than of the file descriptor,
and therefore carries across any
.BR dup (2)ed
file descriptors.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success,
if the file was created
through this file description,
1 is returned;
and if it already existed,
0 is returned.
On error,
\-1 is returned,
and
.I errno
is set to indicate the error.
.SH ERRORS
See
.BR fcntl (2).
.SH STANDARDS
Linux.
.SH HISTORY
Linux 6.12.
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program
repeatedly attempts to open
the file supplied in its first command-line argument,
potentially creating it,
prints whether that file was actually created,
and exits if it wasn't.
Example output:
.P
.in +4n
.EX
.RB $ " ./a.out some-path"
F_CREATED_QUERY: 1 (file was created)
F_CREATED_QUERY: 0 (file exists)
.EE
.in
.SS Program source
.\" SRC BEGIN (created_query.c)
.EX
#define _GNU_SOURCE
#include <err.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
\&
int
main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
	int  fd;
	int  q;
\&
	if (argc != 2)
		errx(EXIT_FAILURE, "Usage: %s path\[rs]n", argv[0]);
\&
	do {
		fd = open(argv[1], O_CREAT | O_RDONLY, 0644);
		if (fd == \-1)
			err(EXIT_FAILURE, "open");
\&
		q = fcntl(fd, F_CREATED_QUERY);
		if (q == \-1)
			err(EXIT_FAILURE, "fcntl");
\&
		printf("F_CREATED_QUERY: %d (file %s)\[rs]n", q,
		       q ? "was created" : "exists");
\&
		close(fd);
	} while (q != 0);
}
.EE
.\" SRC END
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR creat (2),
.BR fcntl (2),
.BR open (2)
